# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-08-phi/blob/main/src/Cred.sol#L638-L648


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
User can prevent the protocol fee from going where it's supposed to go, by reentering the `Cred::_handleTrade()` function

## Proof of Concept
Since in both `isBuy` and `!isBuy` scenarios, some amounts of ether are sent to the `msg.sender`, and since it can be a contract, the function can be reentered. The protocol fee transfer happens after the transfer of funds to the `msg.sender`. This means that he for example can make a couple of deposits and the fee will go where its supposed to go only in the last one, ultimately leaving the `protocolFeeDestination` address without the funds that should be send to it.
The following block of code represents the issue:
```
        if (isBuy) {
            cred.currentSupply += amount_;
            uint256 excessPayment = msg.value - price - protocolFee - creatorFee;
            if (excessPayment > 0) {
                //FIXME: Sending eth means possible reentrancy
                _msgSender().safeTransferETH(excessPayment);
            }
            lastTradeTimestamp[credId_][curator_] = block.timestamp;
        } else {
            cred.currentSupply -= amount_;
            curator_.safeTransferETH(price - protocolFee - creatorFee);
        }

        protocolFeeDestination.safeTransferETH(protocolFee);
        IPhiRewards(phiRewardsAddress).deposit{ value: creatorFee }(
            creator, bytes4(keccak256("CREATOR_ROYALTY_FEE")), ""
        );
        emit Royalty(creator, credId_, creatorFee);

        cred.latestActiveTimestamp = block.timestamp;

        emit Trade(curator_, credId_, isBuy, amount_, price, protocolFee, cred.currentSupply);
    }

```

## Tools Used
Manual Review
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
add the `nonReentrant()` modifier to the `handleTrade()` function





## Assessed type

Reentrancy

# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-08-phi/blob/main/src/PhiFactory.sol#L422-L434


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Wrong implementation of both fees leads to irrelevant fee amounts being added to the actual fee, leading to irrelevant fee payments

## Proof of Concept
As stated in both functions natspec, both values should be denominated in bips and should represent percentage:
```javascript
// @notice Sets the protocol fee percentage.
    /// @param protocolFee_ The new protocol fee percentage (in basis points).
    function setProtocolFee(uint256 protocolFee_) external onlyOwner {
        if (protocolFee_ > 10_000) revert ProtocolFeeTooHigh();
        //FIXME: `mintProtocolFee` irrelevant since it is in bips
        mintProtocolFee = protocolFee_;
        emit ProtocolFeeSet(protocolFee_);
    }

    /// @notice Sets the art creation fee percentage.
    /// @param artCreateFee_ The new art creation fee percentage (in basis points).
    function setArtCreatFee(uint256 artCreateFee_) external onlyOwner {
        if (artCreateFee_ > 10_000) revert ArtCreatFeeTooHigh();
        artCreateFee = artCreateFee_;
        emit ArtCreatFeeSet(artCreateFee_);
    }
```
The implementation in the code is far from that, since the `mintProtocolFee` is charged on the quantity of the arts, rather than on percentage based on somethingas shown in the following block of code:
```javascript
    function getArtMintFee(uint256 artId_, uint256 quantity_) public view returns (uint256) {
        return IPhiRewards(phiRewardsAddress).computeMintReward(quantity_, arts[artId_].mintFee)
            + quantity_ * mintProtocolFee;
    }

```
Same goes for the `artCreateFee` which is charged on an art created from the factory as shown in the `PhiNFT1155::createArtFromFactory()` function:
```javascript
function createArtFromFactory(uint256 artId_) external payable onlyPhiFactory whenNotPaused returns (uint256) {
        _artIdToTokenId[artId_] = tokenIdCounter;
        _tokenIdToArtId[tokenIdCounter] = artId_;

        uint256 artFee = phiFactoryContract.artCreateFee();

        protocolFeeDestination.safeTransferETH(artFee);
        emit ArtCreated(artId_, tokenIdCounter);
        uint256 createdTokenId = tokenIdCounter;

        unchecked {
            tokenIdCounter += 1;
        }
        if ((msg.value - artFee) > 0) {
            _msgSender().safeTransferETH(msg.value - artFee);
        }

        return createdTokenId;
    }
```
## Tools Used
Manual review
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Since both of the fees are charged on art quantity, don't denominate it in bips. Rather than that, denominate it in ether (18 decimals) and change the natspec of both functions


## Assessed type

Context

