# Vulnerability details

## Impact
**`createCred()`** allows users to create new Cred. However, the issue is that the signature lacks a nonce, which means a user can create as many Cred as they want as long as they are within the allowed time range using the same signature.

## Proof of Concept
**`createCred()`** is implemented as follows.
```Solidity
function createCred(
        address creator_,
        bytes calldata signedData_,
        bytes calldata signature_,
        uint16 buyShareRoyalty_,
        uint16 sellShareRoyalty_
    )
        public
        payable
        whenNotPaused
    {
        if (_recoverSigner(keccak256(signedData_), signature_) != phiSignerAddress) revert AddressNotSigned();
        (
            uint256 expiresIn,
            address sender,
            ,
            address bondingCurve,
            string memory credURL,
            string memory credType,
            string memory verificationType,
            bytes32 merkleRoot_
        ) = abi.decode(signedData_, (uint256, address, uint256, address, string, string, string, bytes32));
@>      if (expiresIn <= block.timestamp) revert SignatureExpired();

        if (sender != _msgSender()) revert Unauthorized();

        if (!curatePriceWhitelist[bondingCurve]) revert Unauthorized();

        if (!credType.eq("BASIC") && !credType.eq("ADVANCED")) {
            revert InvalidCredType();
        }
        if (verificationType.eq("MERKLE") && merkleRoot_ == bytes32(0)) {
            revert InvalidMerkleRoot();
        }
        if (!verificationType.eq("MERKLE") && !verificationType.eq("SIGNATURE")) {
            revert InvalidVerificationType();
        }
        if (buyShareRoyalty_ > MAX_ROYALTY_RANGE || sellShareRoyalty_ > MAX_ROYALTY_RANGE) {
            revert InvalidRoyaltyRange();
        }

        uint256 credId = _createCredInternal(
            creator_, credURL, credType, verificationType, bondingCurve, buyShareRoyalty_, sellShareRoyalty_
        );
        if (verificationType.eq("MERKLE")) {
            credsMerkeRoot[credId] = merkleRoot_;
            emit MerkleTreeSetUp(_msgSender(), credId, merkleRoot_);
        }
    }
```
As you can see, the function only checks if **`expiresIn`** is within the valid time range, but it doesn't include a nonce to ensure the signature hasn't been reused. This allows the user to create multiple Cred within the specified time range using the same signature.

To illustrate the issue, copy the following POC into **`Cred.t.sol`**.
```Solidity
function test_createCreds() public {
        vm.warp(START_TIME + 1);

        _createCred("BASIC", "SIGNATURE", 0x0);
        cred.isShareHolder(1, participant);
        _createCred("BASIC", "SIGNATURE", 0x0);
        cred.isShareHolder(2, participant);
        _createCred("BASIC", "SIGNATURE", 0x0);
        cred.isShareHolder(3, participant);

        assertEq(cred.isShareHolder(1, participant), true);
        assertEq(cred.isShareHolder(2, participant), true);
        assertEq(cred.isShareHolder(3, participant), true);
    }
```
As you can see, the user is able to create as many Cred as they want.
