# Vulnerability details

## Impact

The [`distribute` function](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-08-phi/blob/8c0985f7a10b231f916a51af5d506dd6b0c54120/src/reward/CuratorRewardsDistributor.sol#L77) may use a significant amount of gas as it performs a loop over all curator addresses. `distribute` calls `Cred::getCuratorAddresses`. Then the internal function `Cred::_getCuratorData` is called. The size of this [array](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-08-phi/blob/8c0985f7a10b231f916a51af5d506dd6b0c54120/src/Cred.sol#L917) is not bounded even though there is a [supply cap](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-08-phi/blob/8c0985f7a10b231f916a51af5d506dd6b0c54120/src/Cred.sol#L31). The reason is the length of the arrays stored in `shareBalance` never decrease, even when a curator [sells](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-08-phi/blob/8c0985f7a10b231f916a51af5d506dd6b0c54120/src/Cred.sol#L680) all of their shares. Rather than removing the curator's address from the enumerable map, it simply `set`s the value to zero. Unlike the standard Solidity mapping type, the OpenZeppelin enumerable map does not remove the key from the data structure when we set the value at that key to zero. The array lengths stored in `shareBalance` will therefore be equal to the number of addresses to have ever owned shares for the cred ID in question, which is an unbounded number.

Additionally, it may be possible that there are a large number of addresses which hold shares and these addresses have zero balance on the `PhiRewards` contract. Thus, calling `CuratorRewardsDistributor::distribute` will result in a large number of `sstore` operations which set a zero to non-zero value. For example, with 500 addresses whose current `PhiRewards` balance is zero, we incur a gas cost of 500 * 20,000 = 10 million just for these `sstore` operations.

There is a risk that `CuratorRewardsDistributor::distribute` becomes prohibitively expensive to call or otherwise exceeds the gas limit per block, so it can no longer be called. Consequently, the funds would not be recoverable from the `CuratorRewardsDistributor` contract.

